// Every mesh in the file, appended into one vertex and triangle list.
//
// This works now, and what made it work is in blk/one/addr/addr.id: a pointer in
// a .blend is only meaningful inside the run of DATA blocks that follows the ID
// block owning it. Resolved file-globally, every one of the showroom's 29 meshes
// got the first mesh's geometry -- they all record the same `dna_attributes`
// address, because Blender allocates, writes and frees one temporary array per
// mesh and the allocator returns the same pointer each time.
//
// Verified against the user's own 23 MB compressed showroom, with every triangle
// index inside its own vertex list and a bounding box that is a room rather than
// the int range. bl_scene, which walks *objects* rather than meshes, reaches
// 130 657 vertices and 233 552 triangles on the same file -- more than this does,
// because a room puts one ceiling-light mesh in twenty places.
//
// The vertex base offset makes each mesh's own 0-based indices land correctly in
// the shared list. The range guard in bl_tpush stays: a decoder must survive a
// file it does not fully understand, and dropping a triangle beats aborting.
//
// bl_all does not place anything: every mesh arrives in its own local space and
// they pile up at the origin. That is what it is for -- one mesh, or a quick
// count. bl_scene is the one that walks objects and puts each through its own
// world matrix, and it is what the editor and the runtime open a project with.

bl_all(string path) {
  bl_reset();
  int ret_i = bl_all_open(path);
} return int ret_i;

bl_all_open(string path) {
  int bl_open_v = bl_open(path);
  lset((import bl_st), 11, bl_open_v);
  int ret_i = bl_all2();
} return int ret_i;

bl_all2() {
  int i = 0;
  while (i < len((import bl_bc)) && (import bl_st)[11] = 1) {
    bl_one_mesh(i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  int ret_i = len((import bl_ti)) > 0;
} return int ret_i;
